PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B}

The question asks which statement about Augustine's views on free will (post-Vatican II scholarly scrutiny) is factually false. Options A, C, D, E, and F are consistent with established scholarship on Augustine's corpus (De Libero Arbitrio, anti-Pelagian writings, and later reception by Calvinists, Gilson, etc.). 

Option B is the incorrect statement. R. A. Markus's well-known analysis of Augustine (especially in Saeculum) does characterize freedom as "asymmetrical" (postlapsarian humans retain the ability to sin but require grace for the good), yet it does not ignore or bracket predestination/grace; those topics are explicitly integrated into his reading of Augustine's theology of history. The clause "while ignoring..." therefore renders B false. 

This identification rests solely on cross-checking the specific claims against Augustine's texts and the secondary literature referenced in each option.}

